### A create on a tenant-scoped entity carries `tenant_id` itself: nothing stamps it from the
### caller's context, and the synthesized tenant scope's WITH CHECK then judges the value.
# @name CreateAcme
## TEST-EXPECT-STATUS: [201]
POST {{fsBaseUrl}}/api/customers
X-Alvo-Api-Key: {{keyDispatcherNorth}}
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "tenant_id": "{{tenantNorth}}",
  "name": "Acme Manufacturing",
  "tier": "priority",
  "email": "ops@acme.test",
  "phone": "+421 900 123 456",
  "notes": "Two sites, both on the annual contract."
}

### A second customer, so a filter over `customer_id` has something to discriminate
# @name CreateBorovica
## TEST-EXPECT-STATUS: [201]
POST {{fsBaseUrl}}/api/customers
X-Alvo-Api-Key: {{keyDispatcherNorth}}
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "tenant_id": "{{tenantNorth}}",
  "name": "Borovica Bakery",
  "tier": "standard",
  "email": "office@borovica.test"
}

### Omitting `tenant_id` is refused rather than defaulted — a row must not land in a tenant by accident
# @name CreateWithoutTenant
## TEST-EXPECT-STATUS: [403]
POST {{fsBaseUrl}}/api/customers
X-Alvo-Api-Key: {{keyDispatcherNorth}}
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "name": "Tenantless Ltd",
  "tier": "standard"
}

### A dispatcher cannot place a row in a tenant their key was not issued for
# @name CreateIntoAnotherTenant
## TEST-EXPECT-STATUS: [403]
POST {{fsBaseUrl}}/api/customers
X-Alvo-Api-Key: {{keyDispatcherNorth}}
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "tenant_id": "{{tenantSouth}}",
  "name": "Trespass Ltd",
  "tier": "standard"
}

### The state of the world after the two refusals: exactly the two rows that were accepted
# @name ListNorthCustomers
## TEST-EXPECT-STATUS: [200]
GET {{fsBaseUrl}}/api/customers?limit=100&order=name.asc
X-Alvo-Api-Key: {{keyDispatcherNorth}}
